#!/usr/bin/python3
"""AIDE's daily report, without what the host's packages explain, and its baseline.

Managed by Ansible (aide).

aide-report: checks the host's files against the accepted baseline, leaves out
the changes its package manager vouches for, and mails root what remains,
through Postfix. Once the relay Postfix sends through accepts the mail, as
Postfix's log in the journal shows, the files as that check found them become
the baseline, so each change is mailed until a mail of it gets through. A mail
the relay defers or refuses, or that Postfix has logged no answer for within
five minutes, accepts nothing, and the next mail says so. AIDE's --update
compares the files and writes their new database in the same walk, and only
that database is accepted, so a change made after the walk stays for the next
report. A mail accepts nothing where there is no baseline, the check failed,
the report is too long for one mail, or the mail is only of container images,
which are reported until repaired. Each mail begins with when the previous one
was sent, and its subject, so that a mail lost on the way leaves a gap the
next one shows. While an Ansible run is writing on the host, from its
--run-started until its --run-ended, the check is held, and --run-ended runs
it once the run's refresh has moved the baseline for what the run wrote; a
run that marked its start more than RUN_HOLD ago holds nothing, as one that
died before its end.
aide-report --run-started: marks that an Ansible run has started writing.
aide-report --run-ended: clears that mark, after the run's refresh, and starts
the check it held, if it held one.
aide-report --baseline: accepts the files as they are: a new database, and the
list of package-owned paths beside it, which later reports use to tell a path
an upgrade removed from one removed by hand. The list is the packages' before
the walk, so that one a package manager changes while AIDE walks counts as
changed since the baseline.
aide-report --refresh PATH... [--units UNIT...]: moves the baseline for those
paths alone, the files an Ansible run wrote, in which a * stands for any part
of one name, and for the links in /etc/systemd/system of the units it enabled,
disabled or masked, and leaves every other entry as it was.
aide-report --repair-images: removes the damaged images and layers that the
container engine's own check of its image layers finds, with the engine's own
repair, Podman's; images that containers use stay, damaged or not.

A change is left out when:
- the path belongs to an installed package that vouches for it as it is now:
  rpm --verify finds nothing but its mtime, or dpkg --verify finds its contents
  intact and AIDE saw no change in its type, mode, ownership or other metadata
  dpkg does not record; rpm records no contents for a %ghost file, so never
  vouches for one, nor for a file whose package's %verify(not ...) leaves its
  digest unchecked, as setup's does for /etc/passwd, or a change in what else
  it leaves unchecked;
- the path is a symlink of a package, pointing at a file of a package that
  vouches for it;
- the path is a copy in /boot that kernel-install made of a kernel's own file,
  which its package lists there only as a %ghost: it holds what the file it
  copies in /usr/lib/modules holds, or points at it, and that file's package
  vouches for it;
- the path is a cache a tool builds from files AIDE watches, ld.so.cache by
  ldconfig or gconv-modules.cache by iconvconfig, the tool builds the same
  bytes now, into a scratch file, and nothing else about it changed but what
  rebuilding it changes;
- the path is what the install of a package installed or upgraded since the
  baseline made, or what its removal took away, following from a file of the
  package that vouches for it: a unit's links by its unit file's [Install]
  (systemctl enable or preset, or deb-systemd-helper, and its record of them),
  and update-rc.d's links by the init script's LSB runlevels;
- the path is a directory added or removed with what it held: the report
  lists an entry below it in the same section, and leaves out every entry
  below it, as a .wants a package's install made for its links, or that
  disabling the last of them removed, or a directory rpm made for a
  package's files without any package owning it;
- the path is one of update-alternatives': a link in /etc/alternatives that
  a group's record names, or the generic name the record gives it, pointing
  there, that resolves to a file of a package that vouches for it; or the
  record itself, while its master link does;
- the path is dpkg's diversions, or its copy of them, and every diversion
  added or removed since the baseline is a package's that was installed,
  upgraded or removed since;
- the path is dpkg's list of file triggers, and it lists exactly those the
  installed packages' records under /var/lib/dpkg/info declare: dpkg rewrites
  it as it upgrades any package with one, moving that package's lines to the
  end;
- the path is one a kernel's installation generates, named for its release
  (its initramfs images, its boot entry, depmod's indexes of its modules, and
  on Enterprise Linux microcode_ctl's firmware links and weak-modules' links
  for it), added with a kernel package installed since the baseline, or gone
  with one removed since;
- the path is kdump's initramfs for an installed kernel, added and written in
  the minutes after a boot into that kernel that began after the baseline was
  accepted, this boot or one the login records hold: kdump.service builds it
  at the first boot into a kernel, not at the kernel's installation;
- the path is one a package's scriptlets write, which no package vouches for
  as it is (Enterprise Linux's boot entries and /etc/default/grub, which
  grubby rewrites; the initramfs images; GRUB's configuration; Proxmox VE's
  APT settings; python3-idm-pki's record of its version; and the copies kept
  beside a configuration file in /etc as it changes, .orig, .rpmnew,
  .dpkg-old and the like), last written, by its change time, during a package
  transaction since the baseline, as dnf's history or apt's records it;
- the path is a file rpm --verify finds as its package has it but for its
  capabilities, the package was installed or upgraded since the baseline, and
  its own scriptlets name the file, as oracle-cloud-agent's sets those of its
  bastions plugin;
- the path is Java's keystore of trusted certificates, holding exactly the
  certificates of the PEM bundle it is built from, or on Enterprise Linux of
  its keystore extracted again: it records when each was added, so its bytes
  differ at every rebuild;
- the path is snapd's, of a revision of a snap that snapd holds as the store
  signed it: the revision's file, whose SHA3-384 the store's assertion of that
  revision names, for the snap that the store's declaration gives that name;
  that assertion; the directories it is mounted on and keeps its data in; the
  unit that mounts it, read-only, where snapd mounts it, and the links that
  enable that unit; and the snap's current link to it. Or it is gone with a
  revision snapd no longer holds, while the current link points at another;
- the path is snapd's cache of a file it downloaded, named for that file's own
  SHA3-384, which it holds, or gone from it;
- the path is what snapd writes again as it refreshes a snap or starts anew,
  from what the snaps declare (their AppArmor, seccomp and mount profiles,
  udev rules and service units, the copy of a revision's data, and its own
  records under /var/lib/snapd), written within minutes of a refresh since
  the baseline, as the snap's current link dates it, or of snapd's own
  package changing; or gone after such a refresh;
- the path is gone, and the package that owned it at the baseline is no longer
  installed in that version (one removed but not purged is not installed,
  though dpkg keeps its version), or it now lies below a path the configuration
  excludes (not below a new mount point: a filesystem mounted over a watched
  directory hides what was there, which stays in the report);
- the path is one of dpkg's records of a package under /var/lib/dpkg/info, its
  maintainer scripts among them, and the package was installed, upgraded or
  removed since the baseline, or the record's contents are unchanged;
- the path is a directory whose only changes are its timestamps, size, inode
  or link count, which follow from the entries in it, each listed itself;
- the path was rewritten with what it held: AIDE compared its contents, a
  file's hashes or a symlink's target, and found them unchanged, and nothing
  about it changed but its timestamps and inode, and the user of its SELinux
  label, which records who wrote it: a service rewriting with system_u a file
  last written from a login with unconfined_u. A change in its type, role or
  level stays in the report.

dpkg records its files' contents alone, so on Debian and Ubuntu the mode of a
file a package installed since the baseline is not checked. A file dpkg
diverts is its package's where the diversion moved it, where dpkg --verify
checks it.

While the firmware reports Secure Boot on, the paths the settings leave to it,
/boot by default, are excluded too, read from its Secure Boot variable at each
run.

AIDE does not walk the container engines' image stores, which every pull
changes. aide-report runs each engine's own check of its image layers instead,
and mails what it finds with the report:
- Podman's, podman system check from Podman 5.2 and containers-storage check
  before it: each layer rebuilt from Podman's record of it and checked against
  its diff ID, and its files' type, owner, mode, size and mtime, with none
  added or removed. Neither compares setuid, setgid or sticky bits, extended
  attributes or symlink targets;
- Docker's, docker save of each image, on Docker's own image store: each
  layer's file contents, rebuilt and checked against its diff ID;
- none on Docker's containerd image store, which has no check of the layers
  containers run from.

Settings come from a JSON file, by default /etc/aide-report/settings.json.
"""

import argparse
import base64
import datetime
import email.message
import email.policy
import email.utils
import fcntl
import glob
import gzip
import hashlib
import json
import os
import py_compile
import re
import shutil
import socket
import sqlite3
import stat
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time

SETTINGS = "/etc/aide-report/settings.json"

# AIDE's attributes, by the field names of its JSON report. A package's own
# file may show changes in its contents and in what follows from replacing a
# file; dpkg vouches for those alone, as it records only its files' contents.
# AIDE 0.19 added the last three hashes.
CHECKSUMS = {
    "md5", "sha1", "sha256", "sha512", "rmd160", "tiger", "haval", "gost",
    "crc32", "crc32b", "whirlpool", "stribog256", "stribog512",
    "sha512-256", "sha3-256", "sha3-512",
}
CONTENT = CHECKSUMS | {"size", "block_count", "growing_size"}
INCIDENTAL = {"access_time", "change_time", "modify_time", "inode", "link_count"}
# rpm --verify also checks mode, ownership, link target, type and
# capabilities; neither package manager records these.
UNRECORDED = {"acl", "selinux", "xattrs", "e2fsattrs"}
# What rpm --verify checks of a file, by the bits of its verify flags
# (RPMVERIFY_* in rpm's rpmfiles.h), each with the attributes of AIDE's report
# it covers. A package's %verify(not ...) clears some for a file, as setup's
# does the digest, size and mtime of /etc/passwd, and rpm --verify then passes
# whatever the file holds there. The mtime (1 << 5) is not among them, as
# rpm's T alone leaves a file as its package has it, nor the device numbers
# (1 << 7), which AIDE does not record.
RPM_VERIFIES = (
    (1 << 0, CHECKSUMS),                                    # file digest
    (1 << 1, {"size", "block_count", "growing_size"}),      # size
    (1 << 2, {"link_name"}),                                # link target
    (1 << 3, {"uid"}),                                      # user
    (1 << 4, {"gid"}),                                      # group
    (1 << 6, {"perm", "file_type"}),                        # mode
    (1 << 8, {"capabilities"}),                             # capabilities
)
# dpkg's records of a package: /var/lib/dpkg/info/<package>[:<arch>].<kind>.
DPKG_INFO = re.compile(r"^/var/lib/dpkg/info/([^/]+)\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$")
# The links of a merged /usr, which AIDE does not follow. Packages may still
# list their files below them, as dpkg records each path as its package has it.
USR_MERGE_LINKS = ("/bin", "/sbin", "/lib", "/lib32", "/lib64", "/libx32")
# What rewriting a file with what it already held changes.
REWRITTEN = {"access_time", "change_time", "modify_time", "inode"}
# The firmware's Secure Boot variable: four bytes of attributes, then 1 while
# Secure Boot is on.
SECURE_BOOT_VARIABLE = "/sys/firmware/efi/efivars/SecureBoot-8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c"
# The copies kernel-install makes in /boot of a kernel's own files, each with
# the file of its package it copies. The package lists the copies as %ghost.
KERNEL_COPIES = (
    (re.compile(r"^/boot/(vmlinuz|System\.map|config)-([^/]+)$"), r"/usr/lib/modules/\2/\1"),
    (re.compile(r"^/boot/\.vmlinuz-([^/]+)\.hmac$"), r"/usr/lib/modules/\1/.vmlinuz.hmac"),
    (re.compile(r"^/boot/symvers-([^/]+)\.gz$"), r"/usr/lib/modules/\1/symvers.gz"),
    # The arm64 UEK's device trees, which grubby's devicetree plugin copies.
    (re.compile(r"^/boot/dtb-([^/]+)/(.+)$"), r"/usr/lib/modules/\1/dtb/\2"),
)
# The links a kernel's installation or removal points at one of the installed
# kernels' files, each with the targets it may have: the arm64 UEK's
# /boot/dtb, which grubby's devicetree plugin points at each kernel it adds;
# Debian's and Ubuntu's /boot links to the newest kernel and the one before,
# from linux-base's linux-update-symlinks; and kdump-tools' links to the image
# and initramfs it loads.
KERNEL_LINKS = {
    "/boot/dtb": re.compile(r"^dtb-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/boot/vmlinuz": re.compile(r"^vmlinuz-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/boot/vmlinuz.old": re.compile(r"^vmlinuz-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/boot/initrd.img": re.compile(r"^initrd\.img-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/boot/initrd.img.old": re.compile(r"^initrd\.img-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/var/lib/kdump/vmlinuz": re.compile(r"^/boot/vmlinuz-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
    "/var/lib/kdump/initrd.img": re.compile(r"^/var/lib/kdump/initrd\.img-(?P<release>[^/]+)$"),
}
# A kernel's image, by which its package is known.
KERNEL_IMAGE = re.compile(r"^(/usr/lib/modules/[^/]+/vmlinuz|/boot/vmlinuz-[^/]+)$")
# What a kernel's installation generates, named for its release: its initramfs
# images (dracut's and kdump's, or initramfs-tools' and its record of one), its
# boot entry, depmod's indexes of its modules, and the directory of its keys.
# On Enterprise Linux also microcode_ctl's tree for it in /usr/lib/firmware,
# links to the microcode it allows that kernel and their readmes, and
# weak-modules' links to the kABI-compatible modules another kernel's kmod
# packages installed. On Debian and Ubuntu also kdump-tools' initramfs for it
# and its records of the memory it reserves and the kernel's settings, and the
# trigger that runs linux-update-symlinks once the kernel's packages are in.
# DKMS's modules built for a kernel stay in the report: nothing vouches for them.
KERNEL_GENERATED = tuple(re.compile(pattern) for pattern in (
    r"^/boot/initramfs-(?P<release>[^/]+?)(kdump)?\.img$",
    r"^/boot/initrd\.img-(?P<release>[^/]+)$",
    r"^/var/lib/initramfs-tools/(?P<release>[^/]+)$",
    r"^/boot/loader/entries/[0-9a-f]{32}-(?P<release>[^/]+)\.conf$",
    r"^/usr/lib/modules/(?P<release>[^/]+)/modules\.[^/]+$",
    r"^/usr/share/doc/kernel-keys/(?P<release>[^/]+)$",
    r"^/usr/lib/firmware/(?P<release>[^/]+)(/.*)?$",
    r"^/usr/lib/modules/(?P<release>[^/]+)/weak-updates/.+$",
    r"^/var/lib/kdump/(initrd\.img|size_initrd\.img|mem|latest_sysctls)-(?P<release>[^/]+)$",
    r"^/var/lib/dpkg/triggers/linux-update-(?P<release>[^/]+)$",
))
# kdump's initramfs for a kernel, which kdump.service builds at the first boot
# into that kernel: kexec-tools' kernel-install hook builds none. It builds it
# in the minutes after the boot begins, within KDUMP_BUILT seconds of it.
KDUMP_IMAGE = re.compile(r"^/boot/initramfs-(?P<release>[^/]+)kdump\.img$")
KDUMP_BUILT = 900
# The login records, the older first, in which each boot is a record of glibc's
# struct utmp on 64-bit Linux, of type BOOT_TIME, with the kernel's release.
WTMP = ("/var/log/wtmp.1", "/var/log/wtmp")
UTMP_RECORD = struct.Struct("@hi32s4s32s256shhiii4i20s")
UTMP_BOOT_TIME = 2
# What a package's scriptlets write as it is installed, upgraded or removed,
# which no package vouches for as it is: Enterprise Linux's boot entries and
# /etc/default/grub, which grubby rewrites as a kernel is installed and as
# oci-linux-config's scriptlet adds its arguments to every kernel's; the
# initramfs images dracut and initramfs-tools build again as a package with a
# module for them changes; GRUB's configuration, which update-grub and
# grub2-mkconfig write; Proxmox VE's APT settings, which pve-manager's
# scriptlet writes at each upgrade; the version python3-idm-pki's scriptlet
# records of itself; and the copies in /etc that scriptlets and the package
# managers keep beside a configuration file they change, as ipa-client's
# trigger does with sed --in-place=.orig, rpm of a file it would replace or
# remove, and dpkg and ucf of a configuration file whose packaged version
# changed. Each is left out where it was last written during a package
# transaction since the baseline, as dnf's history or apt's records it, or
# within TRANSACTION_TAIL seconds of its end.
TRANSACTION_WRITTEN = tuple(re.compile(pattern) for pattern in (
    r"^/boot/loader/entries/[0-9a-f]{32}-[^/]+\.conf$",
    r"^/etc/default/grub$",
    r"^/boot/initramfs-[^/]+\.img$",
    r"^/boot/initrd\.img-[^/]+$",
    r"^/boot/grub2?/grub\.cfg$",
    r"^/etc/apt/apt\.conf\.d/76pveconf$",
    r"^/etc/pki/pki\.version$",
    r"^/etc/.+\.(orig|rpmnew|rpmsave|dpkg-dist|dpkg-old|dpkg-new|ucf-dist|ucf-old)$",
))
TRANSACTION_TAIL = 60
DNF_HISTORY = "/var/lib/dnf/history.sqlite"
APT_HISTORY = "/var/log/apt/history.log"
# snapd's own records of the snaps it holds: each revision's file, named for
# the snap and the revision; the store's signed assertion of each revision, in
# a directory named for the base64 of its file's SHA3-384, and of each snap's
# name, by its ID; and its cache of the files it downloaded, each named for
# the hex of its own SHA3-384.
SNAPD = "/var/lib/snapd"
SNAP_FILES = SNAPD + "/snaps"
SNAP_CACHE = SNAPD + "/cache"
SNAP_REVISIONS = SNAPD + "/assertions/asserts-v0/snap-revision"
SNAP_DECLARATIONS = SNAPD + "/assertions/asserts-v0/snap-declaration/16"
SNAP_FILE = re.compile(r"^(?P<name>[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*)_(?P<revision>[0-9]+)\.snap$")
SNAP_DIGEST = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{96}$")
# Where each revision of a snap is mounted, and where its data is kept, each
# with a link, current, to the revision in use.
SNAP_ROOTS = ("/snap", "/var/snap", "/root/snap")
SNAP_DATA_ROOTS = ("/var/snap", "/root/snap")
# What snapd writes again as it refreshes a snap, or as a new snapd starts,
# from what the snaps declare: its records of them under /var/lib/snapd, the
# snaps' AppArmor, seccomp and mount profiles among them, their udev rules,
# the units of their services, and each revision's data, copied from the
# revision before. Each is left out where it was written within SNAPD_WINDOW
# seconds of a refresh since the baseline: when a snap's current link was
# pointed at a revision snapd holds and the store signed, or when snapd's own
# package changed, by its daemon's change time.
SNAPD_UDEV_RULES = re.compile(r"^/etc/udev/rules\.d/70-snap\.[^/]+\.rules$")
SNAPD_UNIT = re.compile(r"^snap\.[^/]+\.(?:service|timer|socket)$")
SNAPD_WINDOW = 300
SNAPD_DAEMON = "/usr/lib/snapd/snapd"
# Caches a tool builds from files AIDE watches, each with the command that
# builds it into the file named after the command instead: ldconfig's index of
# the shared libraries, without its auxiliary cache and leaving the libraries'
# links alone, and iconvconfig's of glibc's character-set converters, in the
# native library directory. A multilib's, such as /usr/lib32's, is not one the
# native iconvconfig builds; on Debian and Ubuntu, packages ship both.
REBUILT = (
    (re.compile(r"^/etc/ld\.so\.cache$"), ["ldconfig", "-i", "-X", "-C"]),
    (re.compile(r"^/usr/lib(64|/[^/]+-linux-gnu)/gconv/gconv-modules\.cache$"), ["iconvconfig", "-o"]),
)
# Trees a tool extracts from what AIDE watches, each with the command that
# extracts the same tree into the directory named after it, which must hold the
# tree's directories already: Enterprise Linux's trust store, from
# /usr/share/pki/ca-trust-source and /etc/pki/ca-trust/source. Java's keystore
# in it records when each certificate was extracted, so it differs at every
# extraction: it is compared by the certificates it holds instead.
TRUST_STORE = "/etc/pki/ca-trust/extracted"
EXTRACTED = ((TRUST_STORE, ["update-ca-trust", "extract", "-o"]),)
# Where Enterprise Linux 10's update-ca-trust also links each certificate by
# its subject's hash, to the trust store's own link of that name: the hashed
# directory OpenSSL looks in.
TRUST_LINKS = "/etc/pki/tls/certs"
TRUST_HASHED = TRUST_STORE + "/pem/directory-hash"
TRUST_LINK_NAME = re.compile(r"^[0-9a-f]{8}\.[0-9]+$")
# Java's keystores of trusted certificates, which record when each was added,
# so that one built again differs in its bytes: each with the PEM bundle of
# the certificates it is built from, Debian's and Ubuntu's, which
# ca-certificates-java builds from the system's bundle at each change of it.
# Enterprise Linux's, in its trust store, is compared with its own extracted
# again. Both are in Java's own format, JKS.
JAVA_KEYSTORES = {"/etc/ssl/certs/java/cacerts": "/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt"}
JKS_MAGIC = 0xFEEDFEED
PEM_CERTIFICATE = re.compile(r"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----([A-Za-z0-9+/=\s]*?)-----END CERTIFICATE-----")
# Python's bytecode of a module, in the __pycache__ beside its source, by the
# interpreter's cache tag and the optimization level it was compiled at, as
# Debian's py3compile writes it after each upgrade of a package's modules.
BYTECODE = re.compile(r"^(?P<directory>/.+)/__pycache__/(?P<module>[^/.]+)\.(?P<tag>[^./]+)"
                      r"(?:\.opt-(?P<optimize>[12]))?\.pyc$")
# A changed entry's summary has a column per attribute, in AIDE's order from
# 0.18 (report_attrs_order in src/report.c): '.' where AIDE compared it at both
# ends and found it unchanged, a space where the entry's rule does not record it.
SUMMARY_LINK_NAME = 1
SUMMARY_HASHES = 12
# Where systemctl enable, disable, mask and link make and remove a unit's links,
# and the suffixes of the unit types; a unit named without one is a service.
SYSTEMD_UNITS = "/etc/systemd/system"
UNIT_SUFFIXES = (".service", ".socket", ".target", ".timer", ".mount", ".automount",
                 ".swap", ".path", ".slice", ".scope", ".device")
# The links enabling a unit makes, by the [Install] settings naming them: a
# link in each target's directory of this suffix, and one of each Alias= name;
# systemctl enables each unit its Also= names with it.
INSTALL_LINKS = {"WantedBy": ".wants", "RequiredBy": ".requires", "UpheldBy": ".upholds"}
# Where systemd looks for the system's unit files that systemctl enables, in
# its order of precedence (systemd.unit(5)), past its runtime and generated
# units, which it does not enable.
UNIT_PATHS = (SYSTEMD_UNITS, "/run/systemd/system", "/usr/local/lib/systemd/system",
              "/usr/lib/systemd/system", "/lib/systemd/system")
# An instance's name, and its template's: foo@bar.service and foo@.service.
UNIT_INSTANCE = re.compile(r"^([^@/]+@)[^@/]+(\.[^.@/]+)$")
# update-rc.d's links to an init script, in each runlevel's directory.
SYSV_LINK = re.compile(r"^/etc/rc([0-6S])\.d/([SK])\d\d([^/]+)$")
# deb-systemd-helper's record of the links it made: a file per unit listing
# them, and an empty file mirroring each.
DSH_STATE = "/var/lib/systemd/deb-systemd-helper-enabled"
# dpkg's diversions, and its copy of them before their last change.
DPKG_DIVERSIONS = "/var/lib/dpkg/diversions"
DPKG_DIVERSIONS_OLD = "/var/lib/dpkg/diversions-old"
# dpkg's list of file triggers, a line for each path a package's records
# declare an interest in: the path, the package, and /noawait for an
# interest-noawait. The records are /var/lib/dpkg/info/<package>.triggers.
DPKG_FILE_TRIGGERS = "/var/lib/dpkg/triggers/File"
DPKG_TRIGGER_RECORDS = "/var/lib/dpkg/info/*.triggers"
# update-alternatives' links, a generic name pointing at its link here, which
# points at the alternative chosen; and its records of each group, dpkg's and
# Enterprise Linux's alike: the group's status, its master link, a name and a
# link for each follower, a blank line, then the alternatives.
ALTERNATIVES = "/etc/alternatives"
ALTERNATIVES_RECORDS = ("/var/lib/dpkg/alternatives", "/var/lib/alternatives")
# Lines of the report in each mail, at most.
MAIL_LINES = 400
# Seconds a run's mark holds the daily check: a fleet run takes an hour or two,
# and one marked longer ago than this died before its end.
RUN_HOLD = 6 * 3600
# The unit the timer starts for the daily check, which --run-ended starts
# for a check it held.
CHECK_UNIT = "aide-report.service"
# How a mail is written: lines of up to SMTP's 998 characters go unencoded, as
# 7bit or, with other than ASCII in them, 8bit; only a longer one is encoded.
MAIL_POLICY = email.policy.default.clone(max_line_length=998)
# How long a check waits for the relay's answer to its mail, and how often it
# reads Postfix's log meanwhile, in seconds. Postfix hands a mail on within
# seconds, and gives up on a relay it cannot reach within a minute or two.
RELAY_WAIT = 300
RELAY_POLL = 5
# Postfix's log, under its default syslog_name, which mail_relay keeps: the
# queue ID cleanup gives a mail, by its Message-ID, and the answer a delivery
# agent logs for a recipient of it: smtp's, or error's while Postfix holds the
# relay unreachable.
POSTFIX_IDENTIFIER = "postfix/"
POSTFIX_QUEUED = re.compile(r"^(?P<queue_id>\w+): message-id=(?P<message_id>\S+)$")
POSTFIX_ANSWER = re.compile(r"^(?P<queue_id>\w+): to=<[^>]*>, .*\bstatus=(?P<status>\w+)(?: \((?P<detail>.*)\))?$")


def load(path):
    with open(path) as handle:
        return json.load(handle)


def unescape(field):
    """A mountinfo field, with its octal escapes decoded."""
    return re.sub(r"\\([0-7]{3})", lambda m: chr(int(m.group(1), 8)), field)


def regex(path):
    """A path as AIDE's regular expressions read it, literally."""
    out = []
    for char in path:
        if char.isalnum() or char in "/_-@+,:~%":
            out.append(char)
        elif char.isprintable() and not char.isspace():
            out.append("\\" + char)
        else:
            out.append("\\x%02x" % ord(char) if ord(char) < 256 else ".")
    return "".join(out)


def mount_points(settings):
    """Every mount point but the watched filesystems'."""
    points = set()
    with open("/proc/self/mountinfo") as handle:
        for line in handle:
            points.add(unescape(line.split()[4]))
    return points - set(settings["filesystems"])


def swap_files():
    files = set()
    try:
        with open("/proc/swaps") as handle:
            next(handle, None)
            for line in handle:
                fields = line.split()
                if len(fields) > 1 and fields[1] == "file":
                    files.add(unescape(fields[0]))
    except OSError:
        pass
    return files


def secure_boot():
    """Whether the firmware reports Secure Boot on."""
    try:
        with open(SECURE_BOOT_VARIABLE, "rb") as handle:
            data = handle.read()
    except OSError:
        return False
    return len(data) >= 5 and data[4] == 1


def excluded_paths(settings):
    """The paths the settings exclude, with those they leave to Secure Boot while it is on."""
    paths = list(settings.get("excluded", []))
    if secure_boot():
        paths += settings.get("excluded_secure_boot", [])
    return [path.rstrip("/") for path in paths]


def below(path, roots):
    """Whether path is one of roots, or below one; / is never such a root."""
    return any(root != "/" and (path == root or path.startswith(root + "/")) for root in roots)


def prune_prefix():
    """The negative rule that keeps AIDE out of a directory altogether: from
    0.19, "!" still walks the directory's children and "-" does not; before
    0.19, "!" does not."""
    result = subprocess.run(["aide", "--version"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                            text=True, errors="replace")
    match = re.search(r"AIDE (\d+)\.(\d+)", result.stdout)
    return "!" if match and (int(match.group(1)), int(match.group(2))) < (0, 19) else "-"


def write_exclusions(settings):
    """Exclude, and keep AIDE out of: every mount point but the watched
    filesystems', the paths the settings exclude, and swap files."""
    prune = prune_prefix()
    lines = [
        "# Generated by aide-report at each run, each a rule AIDE does not walk past.",
        "# Every mount point but the watched filesystems', from /proc/self/mountinfo:",
    ]
    lines += ["%s%s(/|$)" % (prune, regex(point)) for point in sorted(mount_points(settings))]
    lines.append("# The paths the host's settings exclude, with those left to Secure Boot while it is on:")
    lines += ["%s%s(/|$)" % (prune, regex(path)) for path in excluded_paths(settings)]
    # The kernel rewrites swap files as it pages, and they can be gigabytes.
    lines.append("# Swap files, from /proc/swaps:")
    lines += ["%s%s$" % (prune, regex(swap)) for swap in sorted(swap_files())]
    path = settings["exclusions"]
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as handle:
        handle.write("\n".join(lines) + "\n")
    os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)


def aide(settings, action):
    write_exclusions(settings)
    return subprocess.run(
        ["aide", "--config", settings["config"], action, "-A", "report_format=json"],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace")


class Packages:
    """The installed packages' paths, by the package manager the host has."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.rpm = os.path.exists("/usr/bin/rpm") and not os.path.exists("/usr/bin/dpkg")
        self.merged = {link: os.path.realpath(link) for link in USR_MERGE_LINKS if os.path.islink(link)}
        self.owner = {}
        self.installed = set()
        # rpm's %ghost files: listed, with no contents rpm --verify checks.
        self.ghost = set()
        # The attributes of AIDE's report that rpm --verify does not check of
        # a path, by its owner's verify flags, where there are any.
        self.unchecked = {}
        # The paths rpm --verify found as their packages have them but for
        # their capabilities, of those it was asked about.
        self.capabilities_only = set()
        self.scripts = {}
        if self.rpm:
            out = subprocess.run(
                ["rpm", "-qa", "--qf",
                 "[%{FILENAMES}\\t%{FILEFLAGS:fflags}\\t%{FILEVERIFYFLAGS}"
                 "\\t%{=NAME}-%{=VERSION}-%{=RELEASE}.%{=ARCH}\\n]"],
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace", check=True).stdout
            regular = set()
            for line in out.splitlines():
                fields = line.rsplit("\t", 3)
                if len(fields) == 4 and fields[0] and fields[2].lstrip("-").isdigit():
                    path, flags, verify, package = self.canonical(fields[0]), fields[1], int(fields[2]), fields[3]
                    self.installed.add(package)
                    (self.ghost if "g" in flags else regular).add(path)
                    if self.owner.setdefault(path, package) != package or "g" in flags:
                        continue
                    unchecked = frozenset().union(*(names for bit, names in RPM_VERIFIES if not verify & bit))
                    if unchecked:
                        self.unchecked[path] = unchecked
            # A path one package lists as a ghost and another installs is the latter's.
            self.ghost -= regular
        else:
            out = subprocess.run(
                ["dpkg-query", "-W", "-f",
                 "${binary:Package}\\t${Version}\\t${db:Status-Status}\\t${Architecture-Variant}\\n"],
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace", check=True).stdout
            # A package removed but not purged keeps its version, and a record
            # of the configuration files it leaves, as config-files: it is not
            # installed, and dpkg --verify vouches for none of what it left.
            # A package installed as an architecture variant's build, as
            # Ubuntu's amd64v3 packages are, has the variant after its version
            # and a "/", which no version holds: moving to the variant's build
            # of the same version changes the package, as an upgrade does. A
            # dpkg that knows no variants prints the field empty.
            versions = {}
            for line in out.splitlines():
                fields = line.split("\t")
                if len(fields) == 4 and fields[2] not in ("config-files", "not-installed"):
                    versions[fields[0]] = fields[1] + ("/" + fields[3] if fields[3] else "")
            # A file dpkg diverts is where the diversion moved it, which its
            # package's list does not say: there dpkg --verify checks it.
            # Where the package that diverts it installs a file of the same
            # name, that file is its own.
            diverted = {self.canonical(original): (self.canonical(moved), by)
                        for original, moved, by in diversions(DPKG_DIVERSIONS) or ()}
            info = "/var/lib/dpkg/info"
            for name in os.listdir(info):
                if not name.endswith(".list"):
                    continue
                package = name[:-5]
                version = versions.get(package) or versions.get(package.split(":")[0])
                if version is None:
                    continue
                package_id = "%s=%s" % (package, version)
                self.installed.add(package_id)
                with open(os.path.join(info, name), errors="replace") as handle:
                    for line in handle:
                        path = self.canonical(line.rstrip("\n"))
                        if path in diverted:
                            moved, by = diverted[path]
                            if package.split(":")[0] == by:
                                self.owner[path] = package_id
                            else:
                                self.owner.setdefault(moved, package_id)
                            continue
                        self.owner.setdefault(path, package_id)

    def canonical(self, path):
        """The path AIDE reports for one a package lists, below /usr where merged."""
        top, sep, rest = path[1:].partition("/")
        target = self.merged.get("/" + top)
        return target + sep + rest if target and sep else path

    def versions(self):
        """Each installed package's version, and its variant where it has one,
        by the name dpkg's records use."""
        return dict(package_id.split("=", 1) for package_id in self.installed if "=" in package_id)

    def package(self, package_id):
        """The name the package manager verifies a package by."""
        return package_id if self.rpm else package_id.split("=", 1)[0]

    def unverified(self, paths):
        """The paths, of those given, that their packages do not vouch for."""
        packages = sorted({self.package(self.owner[p]) for p in paths if p in self.owner})
        failed = set()
        for start in range(0, len(packages), 200):
            batch = packages[start:start + 200]
            command = ["rpm", "--verify"] if self.rpm else ["dpkg", "--verify"]
            out = subprocess.run(command + batch, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                 stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, errors="replace").stdout
            for line in out.splitlines():
                match = re.match(r"^(missing|\S{8,9})\s+(?:[cdglr]\s)?\s*(/.*)$", line)
                if not match:
                    continue
                flags, path = match.groups()
                # Only rpm's mtime flag, T, leaves the file as the package has it.
                if self.rpm and flags != "missing" and not set(flags) - set(".T"):
                    continue
                if self.rpm and flags != "missing" and set(flags) - set(".T") == {"P"}:
                    self.capabilities_only.add(self.canonical(path))
                failed.add(self.canonical(path))
        return failed

    def scriptlets(self, package_id):
        """What an rpm package's scriptlets run, as rpm prints them; "" for a
        dpkg package, or where rpm cannot say."""
        if not self.rpm:
            return ""
        if package_id not in self.scripts:
            result = subprocess.run(["rpm", "-q", "--scripts", package_id], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                    stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, errors="replace")
            self.scripts[package_id] = result.stdout if result.returncode == 0 else ""
        return self.scripts[package_id]


def entries(report, section):
    value = report.get(section) or {}
    if isinstance(value, list):
        return {path: "" for path in value}
    return dict(value)


def changed_attributes(report, path):
    return set((report.get("details") or {}).get(path, {}).keys())


def selinux_user_only(report, path):
    """Whether the entry's SELinux label changed in its user alone, as when a
    service (system_u) rewrites a file last written from a login (unconfined_u),
    and its extended attributes in that label alone."""
    details = (report.get("details") or {}).get(path, {})
    label = details.get("selinux")
    if not label:
        return False
    old, new = str(label.get("old", "")).split(":", 1), str(label.get("new", "")).split(":", 1)
    if len(old) != 2 or len(new) != 2 or old[1] != new[1]:
        return False
    xattrs = details.get("xattrs")
    if xattrs:
        def others(values):
            return [value for value in values or [] if " security.selinux = " not in value]
        if others(xattrs.get("old")) != others(xattrs.get("new")):
            return False
    return True


def same_contents(first, second):
    """Whether two files hold the same bytes."""
    try:
        with open(first, "rb") as a, open(second, "rb") as b:
            while True:
                chunk = a.read(1 << 20)
                if chunk != b.read(1 << 20):
                    return False
                if not chunk:
                    return True
    except OSError:
        return False


def rebuilds_same(path, command, scratch):
    """Whether the tool that builds a cache builds the same bytes now, into a
    file in scratch that is removed again."""
    os.makedirs(scratch, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    rebuilt = os.path.join(scratch, "rebuilt")
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(command + [rebuilt], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        return result.returncode == 0 and same_contents(path, rebuilt)
    except OSError:
        return False
    finally:
        try:
            os.remove(rebuilt)
        except OSError:
            pass


# The invalidation modes of Python's bytecode, by the flags in its header.
BYTECODE_MODES = {
    0: py_compile.PycInvalidationMode.TIMESTAMP,
    1: py_compile.PycInvalidationMode.UNCHECKED_HASH,
    3: py_compile.PycInvalidationMode.CHECKED_HASH,
}


def compiles_same(path, source, optimize, scratch):
    """Whether compiling the source now, as the bytecode's header says it was
    compiled, gives the same bytes, in a file in scratch that is removed
    again. Compiling only parses the source; none of it runs."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            header = handle.read(8)
    except OSError:
        return False
    mode = BYTECODE_MODES.get(int.from_bytes(header[4:8], "little")) if len(header) == 8 else None
    if mode is None:
        return False
    os.makedirs(scratch, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    rebuilt = os.path.join(scratch, "rebuilt.pyc")
    try:
        py_compile.compile(source, cfile=rebuilt, dfile=source, doraise=True, optimize=optimize,
                           invalidation_mode=mode)
        return same_contents(path, rebuilt)
    except (py_compile.PyCompileError, OSError, ValueError, SyntaxError):
        return False
    finally:
        try:
            os.remove(rebuilt)
        except OSError:
            pass


def extract_again(root, command, scratch):
    """The tree extracted again into a new directory in scratch, which holds
    the tree's directories first; None where the command fails. The caller
    removes it."""
    os.makedirs(scratch, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    out = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="extracted-", dir=scratch)
    try:
        for top, directories, _ in os.walk(root):
            for name in directories:
                os.makedirs(os.path.join(out, os.path.relpath(os.path.join(top, name), root)), exist_ok=True)
        result = subprocess.run(command + [out], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        if result.returncode == 0:
            return out
    except OSError:
        pass
    shutil.rmtree(out, ignore_errors=True)
    return None


def install_settings(unit_file):
    """A unit file's [Install] settings, each with the list of its values."""
    settings = {}
    section = None
    try:
        with open(unit_file, errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.strip()
                if line.startswith("[") and line.endswith("]"):
                    section = line[1:-1]
                elif section == "Install" and "=" in line and not line.startswith(("#", ";")):
                    key, _, value = line.partition("=")
                    settings.setdefault(key.strip(), []).extend(value.split())
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return settings


def install_links(unit_file, name=None):
    """The links in /etc/systemd/system that enabling a unit makes, from its
    file's [Install] section; name is the unit's, where the file is the
    template of the instance enabled."""
    name = name or os.path.basename(unit_file)
    settings = install_settings(unit_file)
    links = {"%s/%s%s/%s" % (SYSTEMD_UNITS, target, suffix, name)
             for key, suffix in INSTALL_LINKS.items() for target in settings.get(key, [])}
    return links | {"%s/%s" % (SYSTEMD_UNITS, alias) for alias in settings.get("Alias", [])}


def lsb_runlevels(script):
    """An init script's runlevels from its LSB header: Default-Start's and
    Default-Stop's."""
    levels = {"Default-Start": set(), "Default-Stop": set()}
    try:
        with open(script, errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                match = re.match(r"^#\s*(Default-Start|Default-Stop):(.*)$", line)
                if match:
                    levels[match.group(1)].update(match.group(2).split())
    except OSError:
        pass
    return levels["Default-Start"], levels["Default-Stop"]


def diversions(path=DPKG_DIVERSIONS):
    """dpkg's diversions, each the file, where it is diverted to, and the
    package diverting it (: for a local one); None without the file."""
    try:
        with open(path, errors="replace") as handle:
            lines = handle.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    return {tuple(lines[i:i + 3]) for i in range(0, len(lines) - 2, 3)}


def file_triggers():
    """The file triggers dpkg lists, each a line of its list; None without it."""
    try:
        with open(DPKG_FILE_TRIGGERS, errors="replace") as handle:
            return set(handle.read().splitlines())
    except OSError:
        return None


def declared_file_triggers():
    """The file triggers the installed packages' records declare, as dpkg lists
    them; None where a record cannot be read. A removed package's record goes
    with its other records, so only installed packages have one."""
    declared = set()
    for record in glob.glob(DPKG_TRIGGER_RECORDS):
        package = os.path.basename(record)[:-len(".triggers")]
        try:
            with open(record, errors="replace") as handle:
                lines = handle.read().splitlines()
        except OSError:
            return None
        for line in lines:
            # deb-triggers(5): a directive and its trigger, then a comment.
            words = line.split("#", 1)[0].split()
            if (len(words) == 2 and words[0] in ("interest", "interest-await", "interest-noawait")
                    and words[1].startswith("/")):
                declared.add("%s %s%s" % (words[1], package, "/noawait" if words[0] == "interest-noawait" else ""))
    return declared


def alternatives():
    """update-alternatives' generic names, each with the name of its link in
    /etc/alternatives, and its records, each with its group's master link."""
    generic, records = {}, {}
    for directory in ALTERNATIVES_RECORDS:
        try:
            names = sorted(os.listdir(directory))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for name in names:
            record = os.path.join(directory, name)
            try:
                with open(record, errors="replace") as handle:
                    lines = handle.read().split("\n")
            except OSError:
                continue
            header = lines[:lines.index("")] if "" in lines else lines
            if len(header) < 2 or not header[1].startswith("/"):
                continue
            records[record] = header[1]
            generic[header[1]] = name
            for follower, link in zip(header[2::2], header[3::2]):
                generic[link] = follower
    return generic, records


def boot_time():
    """When the running kernel booted, from /proc/stat; None without it."""
    try:
        with open("/proc/stat") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                if line.startswith("btime "):
                    return int(line.split()[1])
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    return None


def kernel_package(release, owner):
    """The package of a kernel release, as the owner of its image."""
    for image in ("/usr/lib/modules/%s/vmlinuz" % release, "/boot/vmlinuz-%s" % release):
        if image in owner:
            return owner[image]
    return None


def boots():
    """Each boot the login records hold, and this one, from /proc/stat, as
    when it began and the release of the kernel it booted."""
    found = []
    for path in WTMP:
        try:
            with open(path, "rb") as handle:
                data = handle.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
        for offset in range(0, len(data) - UTMP_RECORD.size + 1, UTMP_RECORD.size):
            fields = UTMP_RECORD.unpack_from(data, offset)
            if fields[0] == UTMP_BOOT_TIME:
                found.append((fields[9], fields[5].split(b"\0", 1)[0].decode("ascii", "replace")))
    booted = boot_time()
    if booted is not None:
        found.append((booted, os.uname().release))
    return found


def package_transactions(rpm):
    """When each package transaction began and ended, as Unix times: dnf's
    history, or the records apt keeps of each of its runs, the rotated ones
    among them."""
    windows = []
    if rpm:
        try:
            database = sqlite3.connect("file:%s?mode=ro" % DNF_HISTORY, uri=True)
            try:
                rows = database.execute("SELECT dt_begin, dt_end FROM trans").fetchall()
            finally:
                database.close()
        except (sqlite3.Error, OSError):
            return windows
        return [(int(begin), int(end)) for begin, end in rows if begin and end]
    for path in sorted(glob.glob(APT_HISTORY + "*")):
        opener = gzip.open if path.endswith(".gz") else open
        try:
            with opener(path, "rt", errors="replace") as handle:
                began = None
                for line in handle:
                    key, _, value = line.partition(":")
                    if key not in ("Start-Date", "End-Date"):
                        continue
                    try:
                        when = time.mktime(time.strptime(" ".join(value.split()), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"))
                    except (ValueError, OverflowError):
                        continue
                    if key == "Start-Date":
                        began = when
                    elif began is not None:
                        windows.append((began, when))
                        began = None
        except (OSError, EOFError):
            continue
    return windows


def file_digest(path, algorithm):
    """A file's digest, by hashlib's name for the algorithm; None where it
    cannot be read."""
    digest = hashlib.new(algorithm)
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            for chunk in iter(lambda: handle.read(1 << 20), b""):
                digest.update(chunk)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return digest


def assertion_headers(path):
    """A snapd assertion's headers, which come before its body and
    signature; {} where it cannot be read."""
    headers = {}
    try:
        with open(path, errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.rstrip("\n")
                if not line:
                    break
                key, separator, value = line.partition(": ")
                if separator and not line.startswith(" "):
                    headers[key] = value
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return headers


def snap_signed(name, revision):
    """Whether snapd holds that revision of a snap, as the store signed it: its
    file, the assertion of that revision for the file's SHA3-384, and the
    declaration that the assertion's snap ID is the snap of that name."""
    path = os.path.join(SNAP_FILES, "%s_%s.snap" % (name, revision))
    if os.path.islink(path) or not os.path.isfile(path):
        return False
    digest = file_digest(path, "sha3_384")
    if digest is None:
        return False
    key = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(digest.digest()).decode("ascii").rstrip("=")
    assertion = assertion_headers(os.path.join(SNAP_REVISIONS, key, "active"))
    snap_id = assertion.get("snap-id", "")
    if (assertion.get("type") != "snap-revision" or assertion.get("snap-sha3-384") != key
            or assertion.get("snap-revision") != revision or not snap_id or "/" in snap_id or snap_id.startswith(".")):
        return False
    declaration = assertion_headers(os.path.join(SNAP_DECLARATIONS, snap_id, "active"))
    return declaration.get("type") == "snap-declaration" and declaration.get("snap-name") == name


def unit_settings(unit_file, wanted):
    """A unit file's settings in one section, each with the list of its values."""
    settings = {}
    section = None
    try:
        with open(unit_file, errors="replace") as handle:
            for line in handle:
                line = line.strip()
                if line.startswith("[") and line.endswith("]"):
                    section = line[1:-1]
                elif section == wanted and "=" in line and not line.startswith(("#", ";")):
                    key, _, value = line.partition("=")
                    settings.setdefault(key.strip(), []).append(value.strip())
    except OSError:
        return None
    return settings


def snap_mount(unit):
    """The snap and revision a unit of snapd's mounts, from its name, the
    escaped path it mounts; None for any other unit."""
    if not unit.startswith("snap-") or not unit.endswith(".mount"):
        return None
    parts = [re.sub(r"\\x([0-9a-fA-F]{2})", lambda m: chr(int(m.group(1), 16)), part)
             for part in unit[:-len(".mount")].split("-")]
    if len(parts) != 3 or parts[0] != "snap" or not SNAP_FILE.match("%s_%s.snap" % (parts[1], parts[2])):
        return None
    return parts[1], parts[2]


def snap_mounts_only(unit_file, name, revision):
    """Whether a unit of snapd's mounts that revision's file, read-only, where
    snapd mounts it, and does nothing else."""
    mount = unit_settings(unit_file, "Mount")
    service = unit_settings(unit_file, "Service") or {}
    if mount is None or service:
        return False
    return (mount.get("What") == [os.path.join(SNAP_FILES, "%s_%s.snap" % (name, revision))]
            and mount.get("Where") == ["%s/%s/%s" % (SNAP_ROOTS[0], name, revision)] and mount.get("Type") == ["squashfs"]
            and len(mount.get("Options", [])) == 1 and "ro" in mount["Options"][0].split(","))


def keystore_certificates(path):
    """The SHA-256 of each certificate in a JKS keystore of trusted
    certificates; None for any other keystore, or one that cannot be read."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as handle:
            data = handle.read()
        magic, version, count = struct.unpack_from(">III", data, 0)
        if magic != JKS_MAGIC or version != 2:
            return None
        offset, digests = 12, set()
        for _ in range(count):
            (tag,) = struct.unpack_from(">I", data, offset)
            if tag != 2:
                # A private key's entry: not a keystore of trusted certificates.
                return None
            (length,) = struct.unpack_from(">H", data, offset + 4)
            # The alias, then when the certificate was added, which differs.
            offset += 4 + 2 + length + 8
            (length,) = struct.unpack_from(">H", data, offset)
            if data[offset + 2:offset + 2 + length] != b"X.509":
                return None
            offset += 2 + length
            (length,) = struct.unpack_from(">I", data, offset)
            certificate = data[offset + 4:offset + 4 + length]
            if len(certificate) != length:
                return None
            offset += 4 + length
            digests.add(hashlib.sha256(certificate).hexdigest())
        # What remains is the keystore's SHA-1 of the rest.
        return digests if len(data) == offset + 20 else None
    except (OSError, struct.error):
        return None


def pem_certificates(path):
    """The SHA-256 of each certificate in a PEM bundle; None where it cannot be read."""
    try:
        with open(path, errors="replace") as handle:
            text = handle.read()
        return {hashlib.sha256(base64.b64decode("".join(body.split()), validate=True)).hexdigest()
                for body in PEM_CERTIFICATE.findall(text)}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def explain(report, packages, manifest, excluded=(), scratch=None, accepted=None):
    """The report's entries, split into those explained and the rest; caches are
    rebuilt in scratch, where it is given. accepted is when the baseline was,
    as a Unix time."""
    added = entries(report, "added")
    removed = entries(report, "removed")
    changed = entries(report, "changed")
    candidates = [p for p in list(added) + list(changed) if p in packages.owner]
    # A changed symlink of a package is left out when what it points at is a
    # file of a package that vouches for it, so its target is verified too.
    targets = {}
    for path, summary in list(added.items()) + list(changed.items()):
        if summary[:1] == "l" and path in packages.owner:
            targets[path] = os.path.realpath(path)
    # A kernel's copies in /boot are vouched for by the files they copy.
    copies = {}
    for path in list(added) + list(changed):
        for pattern, template in KERNEL_COPIES:
            if pattern.match(path):
                copies[path] = pattern.sub(template, path)
    # What a package's install scripts made, systemctl preset among them, by
    # the packaged unit file or init script each follows from.
    sources = {}
    for path, summary in added.items():
        if summary[:1] == "l" and (path.startswith(SYSTEMD_UNITS + "/") or SYSV_LINK.match(path)):
            sources[path] = packages.canonical(os.path.realpath(path))
        elif summary[:1] == "f" and path.startswith(DSH_STATE + "/"):
            unit = os.path.basename(path)
            unit = unit[:-len(".dsh-also")] if unit.endswith(".dsh-also") else unit
            sources[path] = packages.canonical("/lib/systemd/system/" + unit)
    # update-alternatives' links and records, by the file each resolves to.
    generic, records = alternatives()
    alternative_names = set(generic.values())

    def alternative_target(path):
        name = generic.get(path)
        try:
            if name is not None:
                if os.readlink(path) != os.path.join(ALTERNATIVES, name):
                    return None
            elif os.path.dirname(path) != ALTERNATIVES or os.path.basename(path) not in alternative_names:
                return None
        except OSError:
            return None
        return packages.canonical(os.path.realpath(path))

    chosen = {}
    for path, summary in list(added.items()) + list(changed.items()):
        target = alternative_target(records[path] if path in records else path) if (
            path in records or summary[:1] == "l") else None
        if target is not None:
            chosen[path] = target
    # Python's bytecode, by the source it compiles and the optimization level,
    # where this interpreter compiled it.
    bytecode = {}
    for path in list(added) + list(changed):
        match = BYTECODE.match(path)
        if match and match.group("tag") == sys.implementation.cache_tag:
            bytecode[path] = ("%s/%s.py" % (match.group("directory"), match.group("module")),
                              int(match.group("optimize") or 0))
    unverified = packages.unverified(candidates + [t for t in targets.values() if t in packages.owner]
                                     + [t for t in copies.values() if t in packages.owner]
                                     + [t for t in sources.values() if t in packages.owner]
                                     + [t for t in chosen.values() if t in packages.owner]
                                     + sorted({s for s, _ in bytecode.values() if s in packages.owner}))
    kept = {"added": {}, "removed": {}, "changed": {}}
    explained = 0

    def verified(path):
        # rpm --verify checks none of a ghost's contents, nor of a file whose
        # package clears its digest's verify flag, so passes any.
        return (path in packages.owner and path not in packages.ghost and path not in unverified
                and not packages.unchecked.get(path, frozenset()) & CHECKSUMS)

    def vouched(path):
        if not verified(path):
            return False
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path)
        if packages.rpm:
            return not attributes & (UNRECORDED | packages.unchecked.get(path, frozenset()))
        return not attributes - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL

    def rewritten(path, summary):
        # Its contents compared and the same: a file's hashes, a symlink's target.
        column = SUMMARY_LINK_NAME if summary[:1] == "l" else SUMMARY_HASHES
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - REWRITTEN
        if selinux_user_only(report, path):
            attributes -= {"selinux", "xattrs"}
        return summary[column:column + 1] == "." and not attributes

    def socket_made(path, summary, section):
        # A socket, which holds nothing: made anew by the service listening
        # on it, as it is at each start, owned and with the mode it had, or
        # added with a mode that lets no other user write to it.
        if summary[:1] != "s":
            return False
        if section == "changed":
            return not changed_attributes(report, path) - REWRITTEN
        try:
            mode = os.lstat(path).st_mode
        except OSError:
            return False
        return stat.S_ISSOCK(mode) and not mode & stat.S_IWOTH

    def symlink_vouched(path):
        target = targets.get(path)
        return target is not None and verified(target)

    def swapped(path, summary):
        # A package's file that its install or upgrade since the baseline
        # replaced with a link, or a link with a file, as apt's changelogs
        # move between packages: a link to a file its package vouches for, or
        # a file its package vouches for with a mode that sets no IDs and lets
        # no other user write.
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path)
        owner = packages.owner.get(path)
        if ("file_type" not in attributes or owner is None or owner in baseline_installed
                or attributes - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL - {"file_type", "perm", "link_name"}):
            return False
        if summary[:1] == "l":
            return symlink_vouched(path)
        mode = str(((report.get("details") or {}).get(path, {}).get("perm") or {}).get("new", ""))
        return (summary[:1] == "f" and verified(path) and len(mode) == 10
                and not set(mode) & set("sStT") and mode[8] == "-")

    def copy_vouched(path, summary):
        # Holds what the file it copies holds, or points at it, and nothing
        # else about it changed that its contents do not explain.
        twin = copies.get(path)
        if twin is None or not verified(twin) or changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL:
            return False
        if summary[:1] == "l":
            return os.path.realpath(path) == twin
        return summary[:1] == "f" and same_contents(path, twin)

    # A manifest from before aide-report recorded dpkg's architecture variants
    # names each package without its variant: against it, a package is the
    # one the baseline had if only its variant differs.
    if packages.rpm or manifest.get("variants"):
        unvaried = {}
    else:
        unvaried = {package_id.split("/", 1)[0]: package_id for package_id in packages.installed}
    baseline_owner = {packages.canonical(path): unvaried.get(owner, owner)
                      for path, owner in manifest.get("owner", {}).items()}
    baseline_installed = [unvaried.get(package_id, package_id) for package_id in manifest.get("installed") or ()]
    baseline_installed = baseline_installed or list(set(baseline_owner.values()))

    def cache_rebuilt(path, summary):
        # A cache its tool builds the same now from what AIDE watches, with
        # nothing else about it changed but what rebuilding it changes.
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL
        if selinux_user_only(report, path):
            attributes -= {"selinux", "xattrs"}
        if scratch is None or summary[:1] != "f" or attributes:
            return False
        for pattern, command in REBUILT:
            if pattern.match(path):
                return rebuilds_same(path, command, scratch)
        return False

    def bytecode_rebuilt(path, summary):
        # A module's bytecode that compiling its verified source now gives
        # byte for byte, with nothing else about it changed but what
        # compiling it changes.
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL
        if scratch is None or summary[:1] != "f" or path not in bytecode or attributes:
            return False
        source, optimize = bytecode[path]
        return verified(source) and compiles_same(path, source, optimize, scratch)

    # Each tree its tool extracted again, once, where an entry below it needs it.
    extracted = {}

    def extracted_twin(path):
        # Where the tree extracted again holds the path; None outside such a
        # tree, or where extracting it again failed.
        for root, command in EXTRACTED:
            if below(path, [root]) and path != root:
                if root not in extracted:
                    extracted[root] = None if scratch is None else extract_again(root, command, scratch)
                tree = extracted[root]
                return None if tree is None else os.path.join(tree, os.path.relpath(path, root))
        return None

    def extracted_same(path, summary):
        # A file or link of a tree that extracting it again gives the same,
        # with nothing else about it changed but what extracting it changes.
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name"}
        if selinux_user_only(report, path):
            attributes -= {"selinux", "xattrs"}
        kind = summary[:1]
        twin = extracted_twin(path) if kind in ("f", "l") and not attributes else None
        if twin is None:
            return False
        try:
            if kind == "l":
                return os.path.islink(path) and os.path.islink(twin) and os.readlink(path) == os.readlink(twin)
        except OSError:
            return False
        return (os.path.isfile(path) and not os.path.islink(path) and os.path.isfile(twin)
                and not os.path.islink(twin) and same_contents(path, twin))

    def trust_link(path, summary):
        # Enterprise Linux 10's link to a certificate by its subject's hash,
        # which points where the trust store's own link of that name does,
        # at a certificate, both as extracting the store again gives them.
        if (summary[:1] != "l" or os.path.dirname(path) != TRUST_LINKS
                or not TRUST_LINK_NAME.match(os.path.basename(path))
                or changed_attributes(report, path) - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name", "size", "block_count"}):
            return False
        hashed = os.path.join(TRUST_HASHED, os.path.basename(path))
        try:
            target = os.readlink(path)
        except OSError:
            return False
        certificate = os.path.realpath(hashed)
        return (os.path.dirname(target) == TRUST_HASHED and os.path.realpath(path) == certificate
                and os.path.dirname(certificate) == TRUST_HASHED
                and extracted_same(hashed, "l") and extracted_same(certificate, "f"))

    def extracted_gone(path):
        # Gone from a tree that extracting it again no longer gives it in, or
        # a hash's link gone with the trust store's own.
        if os.path.dirname(path) == TRUST_LINKS and TRUST_LINK_NAME.match(os.path.basename(path)):
            path = os.path.join(TRUST_HASHED, os.path.basename(path))
            if os.path.lexists(path):
                return False
        twin = extracted_twin(path)
        return twin is not None and not os.path.lexists(twin)

    def kernels(owner):
        return {package for path, package in owner.items() if KERNEL_IMAGE.match(path)}

    kernels_changed = kernels(packages.owner) != kernels(baseline_owner)

    def kernel_link(path, summary):
        # A link a kernel's installation or removal since the baseline points
        # at a file of an installed kernel, which is there.
        pattern = KERNEL_LINKS.get(path)
        if (pattern is None or summary[:1] != "l" or not kernels_changed
                or changed_attributes(report, path) - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name", "size", "block_count"}):
            return False
        try:
            match = pattern.match(os.readlink(path))
        except OSError:
            return False
        return (match is not None and os.path.exists(path)
                and kernel_package(match.group("release"), packages.owner) is not None)

    def kernel_generated(path, section):
        # Made with a kernel installed since the baseline, gone with one
        # removed since, or changed with either, as depmod rewrites the
        # indexes a removed kernel's modules leave; a later change to it stays
        # in the report. A package may list it as a ghost, as dracut's
        # initramfs is.
        if path in packages.owner and path not in packages.ghost:
            return False
        for pattern in KERNEL_GENERATED:
            match = pattern.match(path)
            if match:
                release = match.group("release")
                installed = kernel_package(release, packages.owner)
                removed = kernel_package(release, baseline_owner)
                installed = installed is not None and installed not in baseline_installed
                removed = removed is not None and removed not in packages.installed
                return {"added": installed, "removed": removed}.get(section, installed or removed)
        return False

    booted = {}

    def kdump_built(path):
        # kdump's image for a kernel of an installed package, written in the
        # minutes after a boot into that kernel that began after the baseline:
        # kdump.service built it at the first boot into the kernel, this boot
        # or one before it. A later rebuild changes it, and stays in the report.
        match = KDUMP_IMAGE.match(path)
        if (not match or accepted is None
                or kernel_package(match.group("release"), packages.owner) is None):
            return False
        try:
            written = os.path.getmtime(path)
        except OSError:
            return False
        if "boots" not in booted:
            booted["boots"] = boots()
        return any(began > accepted and release == match.group("release") and began <= written <= began + KDUMP_BUILT
                   for began, release in booted["boots"])

    transactions = {}

    def transaction_written(path, summary, section):
        # What a package's scriptlets write, last written during a package
        # transaction since the baseline, by its change time, which no write
        # can set back, with nothing else about it changed but what
        # rewriting it changes.
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL
        if selinux_user_only(report, path):
            attributes -= {"selinux", "xattrs"}
        if (section == "removed" or summary[:1] != "f" or attributes or KDUMP_IMAGE.match(path)
                or not any(pattern.match(path) for pattern in TRANSACTION_WRITTEN)):
            return False
        try:
            written = os.lstat(path).st_ctime
        except OSError:
            return False
        if "windows" not in transactions:
            transactions["windows"] = package_transactions(packages.rpm)
        return ((accepted is None or written >= accepted)
                and any(began <= written <= ended + TRANSACTION_TAIL for began, ended in transactions["windows"]))

    def capabilities_set(path, summary):
        # A file rpm finds as its package has it but for its capabilities,
        # which the package's own scriptlets set, naming the file, at its
        # install or upgrade since the baseline: as oracle-cloud-agent's %post
        # does on its bastions plugin.
        owner = packages.owner.get(path)
        return (packages.rpm and summary[:1] == "f" and owner is not None and owner not in baseline_installed
                and path in packages.capabilities_only and path not in packages.ghost
                and not packages.unchecked.get(path, frozenset()) & CHECKSUMS
                and not changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL - {"xattrs"}
                and path in packages.scriptlets(owner))

    def keystore_same(path, summary):
        # Java's keystore, holding exactly the certificates of what it is
        # built from: the system's PEM bundle, or Enterprise Linux's own
        # keystore extracted again, with nothing else about it changed but
        # what building it again changes.
        theirs = path in JAVA_KEYSTORES or (below(path, [TRUST_STORE]) and path.endswith("/java/cacerts"))
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL
        if selinux_user_only(report, path):
            attributes -= {"selinux", "xattrs"}
        if not theirs or summary[:1] != "f" or attributes or os.path.islink(path):
            return False
        if path in JAVA_KEYSTORES:
            expected = pem_certificates(JAVA_KEYSTORES[path])
        else:
            twin = extracted_twin(path)
            expected = None if twin is None else keystore_certificates(twin)
        stored = keystore_certificates(path)
        return bool(stored) and stored == expected

    signed = {}

    def snap_held(name, revision):
        if (name, revision) not in signed:
            signed[(name, revision)] = snap_signed(name, revision)
        return signed[(name, revision)]

    def snap_current(path):
        # A snap's link to the revision in use: its name and that revision,
        # where snapd holds it as the store signed it; None otherwise.
        name, base = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path)), os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(path))
        if os.path.basename(path) != "current" or base not in SNAP_ROOTS:
            return None
        try:
            revision = os.readlink(path)
        except OSError:
            return None
        return (name, revision) if SNAP_FILE.match("%s_%s.snap" % (name, revision)) and snap_held(name, revision) else None

    def snap_revision_path(path):
        # The snap and revision a path of snapd's is for, and what it is: the
        # revision's file, a directory it is mounted on or keeps its data
        # in, or the unit that mounts it or a link enabling that unit.
        directory, name = os.path.dirname(path), os.path.basename(path)
        match = SNAP_FILE.match(name)
        if directory == SNAP_FILES and match:
            return match.group("name"), match.group("revision"), "file"
        if os.path.dirname(directory) in SNAP_ROOTS and name.isdigit():
            return os.path.basename(directory), name, "directory"
        mount = snap_mount(name)
        if mount and directory == SYSTEMD_UNITS:
            return mount + ("unit",)
        if mount and os.path.dirname(directory) == SYSTEMD_UNITS and directory.endswith((".wants", ".requires")):
            return mount + ("link",)
        return None

    def snap_revision(path, summary, section):
        # What snapd holds of a revision of a snap that it holds as the store
        # signed it, or of one it no longer holds, gone with it.
        found = snap_revision_path(path)
        if found is None:
            return False
        name, revision, kind = found
        if section == "removed":
            current = snap_current(SNAP_ROOTS[0] + "/%s/current" % name)
            return (not os.path.lexists(os.path.join(SNAP_FILES, "%s_%s.snap" % (name, revision)))
                    and current is not None and current[1] != revision)
        attributes = changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL
        if not snap_held(name, revision) or attributes - ({"link_name"} if kind == "link" else set()):
            return False
        if kind == "file":
            return summary[:1] == "f"
        if kind == "directory":
            return summary[:1] == "d"
        if kind == "unit":
            return summary[:1] == "f" and snap_mounts_only(path, name, revision)
        # A link enabling the unit, named for it.
        unit = os.path.join(SYSTEMD_UNITS, os.path.basename(path))
        return summary[:1] == "l" and os.path.realpath(path) == unit and snap_mounts_only(unit, name, revision)

    def snap_revision_assertion(path, summary):
        # The store's assertion of a revision snapd holds, in the directory
        # named for that revision's file's SHA3-384, or the directory itself.
        key = path if os.path.dirname(path) == SNAP_REVISIONS else os.path.dirname(path)
        if os.path.dirname(key) != SNAP_REVISIONS or (key != path and os.path.basename(path) != "active"):
            return False
        if summary[:1] != ("d" if key == path else "f"):
            return False
        assertion = assertion_headers(os.path.join(key, "active"))
        snap_id = assertion.get("snap-id", "")
        if assertion.get("snap-sha3-384") != os.path.basename(key) or not snap_id or "/" in snap_id \
                or snap_id.startswith("."):
            return False
        name = assertion_headers(os.path.join(SNAP_DECLARATIONS, snap_id, "active")).get("snap-name", "")
        return bool(name) and snap_held(name, assertion.get("snap-revision", ""))

    def snap_revision_data(path):
        # Below a revision's data directory, which snapd copies from the
        # revision before as it refreshes the snap: /var/snap/<name>/<revision>/...
        for root in SNAP_DATA_ROOTS:
            if below(path, [root]) and path != root:
                parts = os.path.relpath(path, root).split("/")
                return len(parts) > 2 and parts[1].isdigit()
        return False

    def snap_cache(path, summary, section):
        # snapd's cache of a file it downloaded, named for its own SHA3-384.
        if os.path.dirname(path) != SNAP_CACHE or not SNAP_DIGEST.match(os.path.basename(path)):
            return False
        if section == "removed":
            return True
        if summary[:1] != "f" or changed_attributes(report, path) - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL:
            return False
        digest = file_digest(path, "sha3_384")
        return digest is not None and digest.hexdigest() == os.path.basename(path)

    refreshes = {}

    def snapd_refreshes():
        # When a snap's current link was pointed at a revision snapd holds as
        # the store signed it, and when snapd's own package installed its
        # daemon, where either was since the baseline.
        if "times" not in refreshes:
            times = []
            for path, summary in list(added.items()) + list(changed.items()):
                if summary[:1] == "l" and snap_current(path):
                    try:
                        times.append(os.lstat(path).st_ctime)
                    except OSError:
                        pass
            if not packages.rpm and package_changed("snapd"):
                try:
                    times.append(os.lstat(SNAPD_DAEMON).st_ctime)
                except OSError:
                    pass
            refreshes["times"] = [t for t in times if accepted is None or t >= accepted]
        return refreshes["times"]

    def snapd_written(path, summary, section):
        # What snapd writes again as it refreshes a snap or starts anew,
        # written within minutes of such a refresh since the baseline, or
        # gone with one; never a revision's file, which only the store's
        # assertion vouches for, or its cache, which only its digest does.
        directory = os.path.dirname(path)
        generated = ((below(path, [SNAPD]) and directory not in (SNAP_FILES, SNAP_CACHE))
                     or SNAPD_UDEV_RULES.match(path)
                     or (SNAPD_UNIT.match(os.path.basename(path))
                         and SYSTEMD_UNITS in (directory, os.path.dirname(directory)))
                     or snap_revision_data(path))
        if not generated:
            return False
        times = snapd_refreshes()
        if section == "removed":
            return bool(times)
        try:
            written = os.lstat(path).st_ctime
        except OSError:
            return False
        return any(abs(written - refreshed) <= SNAPD_WINDOW for refreshed in times)

    now = packages.versions() if not packages.rpm else {}
    then = dict(i.split("=", 1) for i in baseline_installed if "=" in i) if not packages.rpm else {}

    def dpkg_record(path, section):
        match = None if packages.rpm else DPKG_INFO.match(path)
        if not match:
            return False
        name = match.group(1)
        if now.get(name) != then.get(name):
            return True
        return section == "changed" and not changed_attributes(report, path) - INCIDENTAL

    def package_changed(name):
        # A dpkg package installed, upgraded or removed since the baseline,
        # by its name with or without its architecture.
        def version(versions):
            return sorted(v for k, v in versions.items() if k == name or k.split(":")[0] == name)
        return version(now) != version(then)

    def installed_with(path):
        # Made by the install of a package installed or upgraded since the
        # baseline, from its own unit file or init script, as it vouches for
        # it: a unit's links by its [Install], deb-systemd-helper's record of
        # them, and update-rc.d's links by the script's LSB runlevels.
        source = sources.get(path)
        if source is None or not verified(source) or packages.owner[source] in baseline_installed:
            return False
        sysv = SYSV_LINK.match(path)
        if sysv:
            level, kind, name = sysv.groups()
            start, stop = lsb_runlevels(source)
            return source == "/etc/init.d/" + name and level in (start if kind == "S" else stop)
        links = install_links(source)
        if not path.startswith(DSH_STATE + "/"):
            return path in links
        if path.endswith(".dsh-also"):
            try:
                with open(path, errors="replace") as handle:
                    listed = set(handle.read().split())
            except OSError:
                return False
            return bool(listed) and listed <= links
        try:
            empty = os.path.getsize(path) == 0
        except OSError:
            return False
        return empty and SYSTEMD_UNITS + path[len(DSH_STATE):] in links

    def removed_with(path, summary):
        # What such an install made, gone with the package that owned its unit
        # file or init script at the baseline: a link, or deb-systemd-helper's
        # record of one.
        sysv = SYSV_LINK.match(path)
        if summary[:1] != ("f" if path.startswith(DSH_STATE + "/") else "l"):
            return False
        if sysv:
            source = "/etc/init.d/" + sysv.group(3)
        elif path.startswith((SYSTEMD_UNITS + "/", DSH_STATE + "/")):
            unit = os.path.basename(path)
            source = packages.canonical("/lib/systemd/system/" + unit.replace(".dsh-also", ""))
        else:
            return False
        owner = baseline_owner.get(source)
        return owner is not None and owner not in packages.installed

    # A diversion added or removed by a package installed, upgraded or removed
    # since the baseline: against the diversions the baseline kept, or before
    # it kept them, dpkg's own copy of those before their last change.
    diverted = None
    if not packages.rpm and DPKG_DIVERSIONS in changed:
        current = diversions()
        before = ({tuple(d) for d in manifest["diversions"]} if "diversions" in manifest
                  else diversions(DPKG_DIVERSIONS_OLD))
        diverted = (current is not None and before is not None
                    and all(package != ":" and package_changed(package) for _, _, package in current ^ before))
    # dpkg's file triggers, as the installed packages' records declare them.
    declared = None
    if not packages.rpm and DPKG_FILE_TRIGGERS in changed:
        listed = file_triggers()
        declared = listed is not None and listed == declared_file_triggers()

    for section, items in (("added", added), ("changed", changed)):
        for path, summary in items.items():
            kind = summary[:1]
            attributes = changed_attributes(report, path)
            if kind == "d" and section == "changed" and not attributes - INCIDENTAL - {"size", "block_count"}:
                explained += 1
            elif section == "changed" and rewritten(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif socket_made(path, summary, section):
                explained += 1
            elif dpkg_record(path, section):
                explained += 1
            elif vouched(path):
                explained += 1
            elif section == "changed" and swapped(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif kind == "l" and symlink_vouched(path) and not attributes - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name", "size", "block_count"}:
                explained += 1
            elif copy_vouched(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif (kind == "l" and path not in records and path in chosen and verified(chosen[path])
                  and not attributes - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name", "size", "block_count"}):
                explained += 1
            elif (path in records and path in chosen and verified(chosen[path])
                  and not attributes - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL):
                explained += 1
            elif section == "changed" and cache_rebuilt(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif bytecode_rebuilt(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif extracted_same(path, summary) or trust_link(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif kernel_link(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif kernel_generated(path, section):
                explained += 1
            elif section == "added" and kdump_built(path):
                explained += 1
            elif transaction_written(path, summary, section):
                explained += 1
            elif section == "changed" and capabilities_set(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif keystore_same(path, summary):
                explained += 1
            elif (snap_revision(path, summary, section) or snap_revision_assertion(path, summary)
                  or snap_cache(path, summary, section)
                  or (kind == "l" and snap_current(path) and not attributes - INCIDENTAL - {"link_name", "size",
                                                                                           "block_count"})
                  or snapd_written(path, summary, section)):
                explained += 1
            elif section == "added" and installed_with(path):
                explained += 1
            elif (section == "changed" and diverted and path in (DPKG_DIVERSIONS, DPKG_DIVERSIONS_OLD)
                  and not attributes - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL):
                explained += 1
            elif (section == "changed" and declared and path == DPKG_FILE_TRIGGERS
                  and not attributes - CONTENT - INCIDENTAL):
                explained += 1
            else:
                kept[section][path] = summary
    for path, summary in removed.items():
        owner = baseline_owner.get(path)
        if ((owner is not None and owner not in packages.installed) or dpkg_record(path, "removed")
                or kernel_generated(path, "removed") or removed_with(path, summary) or below(path, excluded)
                or extracted_gone(path) or summary[:1] == "s" or snap_revision(path, summary, "removed")
                or snap_cache(path, summary, "removed") or snapd_written(path, summary, "removed")):
            explained += 1
        else:
            kept["removed"][path] = summary
    for tree in extracted.values():
        if tree is not None:
            shutil.rmtree(tree, ignore_errors=True)
    # A directory added or removed with what it held, everything below it
    # explained: the deepest first, so that one holding another follows it.
    for section, items in (("added", added), ("removed", removed)):
        for path, summary in sorted(kept[section].items(), key=lambda item: -item[0].count("/")):
            if (summary[:1] == "d" and any(p.startswith(path + "/") for p in items)
                    and not any(p.startswith(path + "/") for listed in kept.values() for p in listed)):
                del kept[section][path]
                explained += 1
    return kept, explained


def failed(command, result):
    output = (result.stdout or "") + (result.stderr or "")
    return (["%s exited %d:" % (command, result.returncode)]
            + ["  " + line for line in output.splitlines()[-20:]] + [""])


def normal_priority():
    # The unit runs at the lowest priority, but Podman's check holds its layer
    # store while it reads, and a container starting waits for it. Lowering a
    # niceness needs CAP_SYS_NICE outside any user namespace, which root in an
    # unprivileged container lacks, so there the check stays at the unit's.
    try:
        os.setpriority(os.PRIO_PROCESS, 0, 0)
    except OSError:
        pass


def podman_command():
    """Podman's check of its image layers, as its arguments, which --repair
    makes its repair; or, where it has none, lines to mail instead of it.
    Neither without Podman."""
    if not shutil.which("podman"):
        return None, []
    # Podman's warnings go to standard error, and may hold digits of their own.
    version = subprocess.run(["podman", "version", "--format", "{{.Client.Version}}"],
                             stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace")
    numbers = tuple(int(number) for number in re.findall(r"\d+", version.stdout)[:2])
    if version.returncode != 0 or len(numbers) < 2:
        return None, failed("podman version", version)
    # Without flags, both check everything; --quick skips the layers' contents.
    if numbers >= (5, 2):
        return ["podman", "system", "check"], []
    if shutil.which("containers-storage"):
        return ["containers-storage", "check"], []
    return None, ["Podman %s has no check of its image layers, and containers-storage, which has one, "
                  "is not installed." % version.stdout.strip(), ""]


def run_podman(argv):
    return subprocess.run(["ionice", "-t", "-c", "2", "-n", "4"] + argv, preexec_fn=normal_priority,
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, errors="replace")


def podman_check(settings):
    """Podman's check of its image layers, as lines to mail, if it found damage."""
    argv, lines = podman_command()
    if argv is None:
        return lines
    result = run_podman(argv)
    if result.returncode == 0:
        return []
    return (["%s exited %d:" % (" ".join(argv), result.returncode)]
            + ["  " + line for line in result.stdout.splitlines()]
            + ["Once each is accounted for, remove the damaged images and layers, but those containers use,",
               "which their services pull again as they start, with:", "  " + settings["repair"], ""])


def repair_images():
    """Podman's repair of what its check of its image layers finds: the damaged
    images that no container uses and the damaged layers, removed. It checks
    again after, and fails if damage is left, as in an image containers use."""
    argv, lines = podman_command()
    if argv is None:
        sys.stderr.write("\n".join(lines or ["There is no Podman here."]) + "\n")
        return 1
    result = run_podman(argv + ["--repair"])
    sys.stdout.write(result.stdout)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        sys.stderr.write("%s exited %d\n" % (" ".join(argv + ["--repair"]), result.returncode))
    return result.returncode


def docker_check():
    """Docker's check of its image layers, as lines to mail, if it found damage."""
    if not shutil.which("dockerd") or subprocess.run(
            ["systemctl", "is-active", "--quiet", "docker.service"]).returncode != 0:
        return []
    info = subprocess.run(["docker", "info", "--format", "{{json .DriverStatus}}"],
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, errors="replace")
    if info.returncode != 0:
        return failed("docker info", info)
    # The containerd image store keeps no record a layer can be rebuilt from.
    if "io.containerd.snapshotter.v1" in info.stdout:
        return []
    listed = subprocess.run(["docker", "image", "ls", "--no-trunc", "--format", "{{.ID}} {{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}"],
                            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True, errors="replace")
    if listed.returncode != 0:
        return failed("docker image ls", listed)
    names = {}
    for line in listed.stdout.splitlines():
        image, _, name = line.partition(" ")
        names.setdefault(image, []).append(name)
    damaged = []
    for image in sorted(names):
        # docker save rebuilds each layer from Docker's record of it and fails
        # on one whose contents no longer match its diff ID. Its output goes to
        # /dev/null as a stream: -o writes a file beside its target and renames it.
        result = subprocess.run(["docker", "save", image], stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace")
        if result.returncode != 0:
            reason = (result.stderr.strip().splitlines() or ["exited %d" % result.returncode])[-1]
            damaged.append("  %s (%s): %s" % (", ".join(names[image]), image, reason))
    if not damaged:
        return []
    return (["docker save could not verify %d images:" % len(damaged)] + damaged
            + ["Once each is accounted for, remove the image and pull it again.", ""])


def previous_mail(settings):
    """The lines each mail begins with: when the previous mail aide-report sent
    from the host was, and its subject, by which to find it in a mailbox; and
    where the relay had not accepted it, what Postfix logged for it."""
    record = os.path.join(settings["state"], "mailed.json")
    try:
        previous = load(record)
        lines = ["The previous mail from aide-report here was sent %s, with the subject: %s" % (
            previous["time"], previous["subject"])]
        # A record without the relay's answer is one from before aide-report
        # waited for it, or from a wait that never ended.
        answer = previous.get("relay", {"status": "sent"})
        unaccepted = "The relay had not accepted it when that check stopped waiting, so it moved no baseline: "
        if answer is None:
            lines.append(unaccepted + "Postfix logged no answer for it within %d minutes." % (RELAY_WAIT // 60))
        elif answer["status"] != "sent":
            lines.append(unaccepted + "%s (%s)" % (answer["status"], answer["detail"]))
        return lines
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return ["No earlier mail from aide-report here is recorded."]
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError, AttributeError) as error:
        return ["The record of the previous mail from aide-report here, %s, cannot be read: %s" % (record, error)]


def record_mail(settings, record):
    os.makedirs(settings["state"], mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    path = os.path.join(settings["state"], "mailed.json")
    with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as handle:
        json.dump(record, handle)
    os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)


def mail(settings, subject, lines):
    """Mails root the report's lines, after the lines naming the previous mail,
    through Postfix's sendmail, which returns once Postfix has queued it, and
    records it as sent: its subject, its Message-ID and the time Postfix took
    it, in ISO 8601 with its UTC offset. A mail sendmail fails raises, and is
    not recorded. Then waits for the relay's answer, records it with the mail,
    and returns whether the relay accepted the mail."""
    if len(lines) > MAIL_LINES:
        lines = lines[:MAIL_LINES] + ["", "... and %d more lines." % (len(lines) - MAIL_LINES)]
    host = socket.getfqdn()
    subject = "AIDE on %s: %s" % (host, subject)
    message = email.message.EmailMessage(policy=MAIL_POLICY)
    message["To"] = "root"
    message["Subject"] = subject
    message["Date"] = email.utils.formatdate(localtime=True)
    message_id = email.utils.make_msgid("aide-report", domain=host)
    message["Message-ID"] = message_id
    message.set_content("\n".join(previous_mail(settings) + [""] + lines) + "\n")
    # Postfix's log is read from a second before, as journalctl reads whole seconds.
    since = int(time.time()) - 1
    sent = datetime.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    subprocess.run(["sendmail", "-i", "root"], input=message.as_bytes(), check=True)
    record = {"time": sent, "subject": subject, "message_id": message_id}
    record_mail(settings, record)
    answer = relay_answer(message_id, since)
    record["relay"] = {"status": answer[0], "detail": answer[1]} if answer else None
    record_mail(settings, record)
    return bool(answer) and answer[0] == "sent"


def postfix_log(since):
    """What Postfix has logged to the journal since then, each entry's message;
    nothing where journalctl fails. The entries of a container's Postfix carry
    the container's name instead."""
    result = subprocess.run(["journalctl", "--since", "@%d" % since, "--output", "json",
                             "--output-fields", "SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER,MESSAGE", "--quiet", "--no-pager"],
                            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, errors="replace")
    messages = []
    for line in result.stdout.splitlines() if result.returncode == 0 else []:
        try:
            entry = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        identifier, message = entry.get("SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER"), entry.get("MESSAGE")
        # journalctl gives a message that is not UTF-8 as an array of its bytes.
        if isinstance(identifier, str) and identifier.startswith(POSTFIX_IDENTIFIER) and isinstance(message, str):
            messages.append(message)
    return messages


def postfix_answer(messages, message_id):
    """The first answer Postfix's log gives for the mail of this Message-ID,
    its status and detail, such as sent and the relay's reply; none yet if it
    gives none."""
    queue_ids = set()
    for message in messages:
        queued = POSTFIX_QUEUED.match(message)
        if queued and queued.group("message_id") == message_id:
            queue_ids.add(queued.group("queue_id"))
            continue
        answer = POSTFIX_ANSWER.match(message)
        if answer and answer.group("queue_id") in queue_ids:
            return answer.group("status"), answer.group("detail") or ""
    return None


def relay_answer(message_id, since):
    """Postfix's answer for the mail of this Message-ID, read from its log
    every RELAY_POLL seconds until it gives one, for up to RELAY_WAIT; none if
    it gives none by then."""
    deadline = time.monotonic() + RELAY_WAIT
    while True:
        answer = postfix_answer(postfix_log(since), message_id)
        if answer is not None or time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return answer
        time.sleep(RELAY_POLL)


def describe(report, path, summary):
    details = (report.get("details") or {}).get(path, {})
    parts = []
    for attribute in sorted(details):
        values = details[attribute]
        if attribute in CHECKSUMS:
            parts.append(attribute)
        else:
            parts.append("%s %s -> %s" % (attribute, values.get("old", "?"), values.get("new", "?")))
    line = "  %s  %s" % (summary or "", path) if summary else "  " + path
    return line + ("\n      " + "; ".join(parts) if parts else "")


def save_kept(settings, kept, explained):
    """What the last report mailed, and how many changes it left out."""
    os.makedirs(settings["state"], mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with open(os.path.join(settings["state"], "kept.json"), "w") as handle:
        json.dump({"time": time.time(), "explained": explained, "kept": kept}, handle)


def check(settings):
    """Mails root what the check found, AIDE's and the container engines',
    and once the relay accepts a mail of the files' changes, accepts the files
    as that check found them. A mail the relay has not accepted fails the run."""
    try:
        subject, lines, status, manifest = check_files(settings)
        # AIDE does not walk the engines' image stores: each checks its own.
        images = podman_check(settings) + docker_check()
        if images:
            subject = ", ".join(part for part in (subject, "container images") if part)
            lines = lines + ([""] if lines else []) + images
        if subject:
            if not mail(settings, subject, lines):
                sys.stderr.write("The relay has not accepted the mail, so the baseline stays where it was; "
                                 "see %s.\n" % os.path.join(settings["state"], "mailed.json"))
                return 1
            # Only after the relay accepted the mail, and its record: if this
            # fails, the next mail lists the same changes again.
            if manifest is not None:
                accept(settings, manifest)
        return status
    finally:
        # The database of the check's walk, where no mail accepted it.
        try:
            os.remove(settings["database_new"])
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass


def acceptance(settings, head, body, manifest):
    """A mail's lines, the report's head and body with what the mail does to
    the baseline between them, and the manifest to accept the files with once
    it is sent: none where the mail cannot hold the whole report, so that
    nothing the report holds is accepted without being mailed."""
    lines = head + ["Once the relay accepts this mail, the baseline moves to the files as this check found them,",
                    "and no later report lists these changes again."] + body
    if len(lines) <= MAIL_LINES:
        return lines, manifest
    return head + ["This mail cannot hold the whole report, so the baseline stays where it was. Once each",
                   "change is accounted for, accept the files as they are with:",
                   "  " + settings["accept"]] + body, None


def check_files(settings):
    """AIDE's report: a mail's subject, or none, its lines, the exit status,
    and the manifest to accept the files with as this check found them once
    the relay accepts the mail, or none. AIDE's --update compares the files and writes
    their new database in the same walk, so a change made after it stays for
    the next report."""
    # A database without the manifest beside it is not one aide-report accepted.
    if not os.path.exists(settings["database"]) or not os.path.exists(settings["manifest"]):
        return "no baseline", ["There is no accepted baseline at %s, so nothing was checked." % settings["database"],
                               "Create one with: " + settings["accept"]], 1, None
    result = aide(settings, "--update")
    if result.returncode >= 14 or result.returncode < 0:
        return ("the check failed", ["aide exited %d." % result.returncode, ""] + result.stderr.splitlines()[-40:],
                1, None)
    if result.returncode == 0:
        save_kept(settings, {"added": {}, "removed": {}, "changed": {}}, 0)
        return None, [], 0, None
    # The packages as the walk left them: what they explain, and the manifest
    # a mail accepts the walk's database with.
    packages = Packages()
    new_manifest = package_manifest(packages)
    try:
        report = json.loads(result.stdout)
    except ValueError as error:
        # AIDE escapes only backslashes, so a path with a quote or a newline in it
        # breaks the JSON: send the report as it is, unfiltered.
        lines, accepted_manifest = acceptance(
            settings, ["AIDE's report is not valid JSON (%s), so nothing was left out." % error],
            [""] + result.stdout.splitlines(), new_manifest)
        return "changes, unfiltered", lines, 0, accepted_manifest
    os.makedirs(settings["state"], mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with open(os.path.join(settings["state"], "report.json"), "w") as handle:
        json.dump(report, handle)
    manifest = {}
    if os.path.exists(settings["manifest"]):
        with gzip.open(settings["manifest"], "rt") as handle:
            manifest = json.load(handle)
    # Only an acceptance writes the manifest, --baseline's or a mail's; a
    # refresh rewrites the database too.
    accepted_at = os.path.getmtime(settings["manifest"])
    kept, explained = explain(report, packages, manifest, excluded_paths(settings),
                              os.path.dirname(settings["exclusions"]), accepted_at)
    save_kept(settings, kept, explained)
    total = sum(len(items) for items in kept.values())
    if total == 0:
        return None, [], 0, None
    accepted = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M %Z", time.localtime(accepted_at))
    head = ["%d changes since the baseline accepted %s that the host's packages do not explain;" % (total, accepted),
            "%d others, which they do or which left a file as it was, were left out." % explained,
            "The full report is %s, and what it mailed %s." % (
                os.path.join(settings["state"], "report.json"), os.path.join(settings["state"], "kept.json"))]
    body = []
    for section in ("added", "removed", "changed"):
        if kept[section]:
            body += ["", "%s (%d):" % (section.capitalize(), len(kept[section]))]
            body.extend(describe(report, path, summary) for path, summary in sorted(kept[section].items()))
    lines, accepted_manifest = acceptance(settings, head, body, new_manifest)
    return "%d changes" % total, lines, 0, accepted_manifest


def package_manifest(packages):
    """What a baseline's manifest records of the host's packages: each
    package-owned path's owner, the packages installed, dpkg's diversions, and
    with dpkg that its packages carry their architecture variants."""
    recorded = {"owner": packages.owner, "installed": sorted(packages.installed)}
    current = None if packages.rpm else diversions()
    if current is not None:
        recorded["diversions"] = sorted(current)
    if not packages.rpm:
        recorded["variants"] = True
    return recorded


def accept(settings, manifest):
    """Makes the database AIDE wrote last the baseline, with the manifest
    beside it, whose mtime is when the baseline was accepted."""
    os.makedirs(settings["state"], mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with gzip.open(settings["manifest"] + ".tmp", "wt") as handle:
        json.dump(manifest, handle)
    os.replace(settings["database_new"], settings["database"])
    os.replace(settings["manifest"] + ".tmp", settings["manifest"])


def baseline(settings):
    # The packages before the walk: one installed or removed while it walks
    # then counts as changed since the baseline, which explains its files.
    manifest = package_manifest(Packages())
    result = aide(settings, "--init")
    if result.returncode != 0:
        sys.stderr.write(result.stderr)
        sys.stderr.write("aide --init exited %d\n" % result.returncode)
        return 1
    accept(settings, manifest)
    print("Accepted the files as they are: %s" % settings["database"])
    return 0


def unit_file(unit):
    """The file systemctl enables a unit from, with its links followed: the
    first of the unit's name along systemd's search path that is no mask, or
    for an instance with none of its own, its template's; or None."""
    instance = UNIT_INSTANCE.match(unit)
    for name in [unit] + (["%s%s" % instance.groups()] if instance else []):
        for directory in UNIT_PATHS:
            path = os.path.join(directory, name)
            # A mask links to /dev/null, which is no file.
            if os.path.isfile(path):
                return os.path.realpath(path)
    return None


def unit_links(units):
    """AIDE's expressions for the links of these units in /etc/systemd/system,
    there now or at the baseline: the unit's own name there, a mask or a link,
    and its name in a .wants, .requires or .upholds directory; and the links
    its file's [Install] names, its Alias= names among them; with the
    directory of each, there now or at the baseline, which enabling it may
    have made, or disabling the last link in it removed. A unit an Also=
    names, which systemctl enables and disables with the one naming it, gets
    the same."""
    patterns = []
    pending = [unit if unit.endswith(UNIT_SUFFIXES) else unit + ".service" for unit in units]
    seen = set()
    while pending:
        unit = pending.pop()
        if unit in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(unit)
        patterns.append("%s/([^/]+\\.(wants|requires|upholds)/)?%s" % (regex(SYSTEMD_UNITS), regex(unit)))
        for kind in ("wants", "requires", "upholds"):
            for link in glob.glob("%s/*.%s/%s" % (glob.escape(SYSTEMD_UNITS), kind, glob.escape(unit))):
                patterns.append(regex(os.path.dirname(link)))
        source = unit_file(unit)
        if source is None:
            continue
        # An instance's links are named for it, an alias's for the unit it names.
        instance = UNIT_INSTANCE.match(unit)
        name = unit if instance and os.path.basename(source) == "%s%s" % instance.groups() else None
        for link in install_links(source, name):
            patterns.append(regex(link))
            if os.path.dirname(link) != SYSTEMD_UNITS:
                patterns.append(regex(os.path.dirname(link)))
        pending.extend(install_settings(source).get("Also", []))
    return patterns


def refresh_pattern(path):
    """A path given to --refresh as AIDE's regular expressions read it: a *
    stands for any part of one name, there now or at the baseline, and every
    other character for itself."""
    return "[^/]*".join(regex(part) for part in path.split("*"))


def refresh(settings, paths, units=()):
    if not os.path.exists(settings["database"]):
        return 0
    write_exclusions(settings)
    # AIDE anchors --limit at the start of each path.
    patterns = [refresh_pattern(path) for path in sorted(set(paths))] + unit_links(units)
    if not patterns:
        return 0
    limit = "(%s)$" % "|".join(sorted(set(patterns)))
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["aide", "--config", settings["config"], "--update", "--limit", limit, "-A", "report_format=json"],
        stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, text=True, errors="replace")
    if result.returncode >= 14 or result.returncode < 0:
        sys.stderr.write(result.stderr)
        sys.stderr.write("aide --update exited %d\n" % result.returncode)
        return 1
    os.replace(settings["database_new"], settings["database"])
    print("Refreshed the baseline for %d paths and the links of %d units." % (len(set(paths)), len(set(units))))
    return 0


def run_mark(settings):
    return os.path.join(settings["run"], "run-pending")


def held_mark(settings):
    return os.path.join(settings["run"], "check-held")


def run_pending(settings):
    """Whether an Ansible run marked its start here less than RUN_HOLD ago and
    has not ended."""
    try:
        return time.time() - os.path.getmtime(run_mark(settings)) < RUN_HOLD
    except OSError:
        return False


def mark(path):
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
    with open(path, "w"):
        pass


def run_started(settings):
    mark(run_mark(settings))
    return 0


def hold_check(settings):
    """Leaves the check to the run's end, which runs it after the refresh."""
    mark(held_mark(settings))
    print("An Ansible run is writing on this host, so the check waits for its end.")
    return 0


def run_ended(settings):
    """Clears the run's mark and starts the check it held, if it held one."""
    try:
        os.remove(run_mark(settings))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    try:
        os.remove(held_mark(settings))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return 0
    result = subprocess.run(["systemctl", "start", "--no-block", CHECK_UNIT])
    if result.returncode == 0:
        print("Started the check the run held.")
    return result.returncode


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.split("\n\n")[0])
    action = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    action.add_argument("--run-started", action="store_true",
                        help="hold the daily check until --run-ended: an Ansible run is writing")
    action.add_argument("--run-ended", action="store_true",
                        help="after the run's refresh, end the hold and start the check it held")
    action.add_argument("--baseline", action="store_true", help="accept the files as they are")
    action.add_argument("--refresh", metavar="PATH", nargs="*", help="move the baseline for these paths alone, a * standing for any part of one name")
    action.add_argument("--repair-images", action="store_true",
                        help="remove the damaged images no container uses, and the damaged layers")
    parser.add_argument("--units", metavar="UNIT", nargs="+", default=[],
                        help="with --refresh, and for the links of these systemd units")
    parser.add_argument("--settings", default=SETTINGS)
    args = parser.parse_args()
    settings = load(args.settings)
    os.umask(0o077)
    # Not behind the lock, which a check holds for minutes as it walks.
    if args.run_started:
        return run_started(settings)
    if args.run_ended:
        return run_ended(settings)
    with open(settings["lock"], "w") as lock:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        if args.baseline:
            return baseline(settings)
        if args.refresh is not None:
            return refresh(settings, args.refresh, args.units)
        if args.repair_images:
            return repair_images()
        if run_pending(settings):
            return hold_check(settings)
        return check(settings)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
